  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCurrently, S/WV mid level ridge moving east across the Northern\nPlains, ahead of a large area of upper level low pressure across\nthe western CONUS. Surface ridge of high pressure extends from\nsouthern Saskatchewan southeast across the Dakotas, with a\nrelatively light west/northwesterly wind across western and\ncentral North Dakota. Extensive area of low level stratus\nremains over most of the area, partially clearing along the\nND/South Dakota border counties and over my far northwest and\nfar north central counties. \n\nFor tonight, models maintain for the most part low level\nstratus as is, with some redevelopment favored southwest, then\nbecoming widespread again as reinforced boundary layer moisture\nadvects north 06-12Z from South Dakota once southerly flow \nincreases. RAP and RRFS hint at some fog Friday morning as well,\nbut for now kept this out of the gridded forecast. \n\nMain highlights for the Friday/weekend timeframe will be \nanother round of precipitation followed by cooling temperatures.\nTonight/early Friday has a split flow regime in place for the \nwestern CONUS, with the aforementioned upper low digging into \nthe Desert Southwest and a northern stream S/WV developing south\nalong the Alberta Rockies. Strong southwest flow aloft develops\nacross the Northern Plains region tonight into Friday, along \nwith a lee side sfc trough developing from eastern Colorado \nnortheast through the central Dakotas. Lead embedded waves will\ncontribute to low chances for rain Friday morning and aft, then\nsome better chances Friday evening as the main piece of energy \nlifts northeast across the region. Still expecting mostly\nliquid until colder air filters into western ND late in the aft\nand evening west of the sfc trough. Little if any accumulation\nstill expected, with perhaps a few hundreths up to around a \ntenth of moisture (highest far north central and northeast), and\nperhaps a tenth or 2 of an inch of snow, though most will see\nnothing on the ground.\n\nThe aforementioned northern stream S/WV dives southward into \nfar southern SK by Saturday morning, then slowly develops east \nalong the International Border area through Sunday. This may \ncontribute to low chances for light snow over the northern half\nof the state this weekend. The main impact of the northern \nstream wave will be much colder temperatures pushing into the\nregion, as a secondary cold front brings a reinforced shot of \ncolder air from Canada. Coldest period is still looking to be \nSunday and Monday as a large area of Canadian high pressure\ndevelops into the region, with highs forecast in the low 20s to\nlow 30s both days, along with overnight lows both Sun and Mon in\nthe teens. \n\nThereafter, progressive flow pattern persists, with our next\nbest chance for precipitation (favored as snow) on Tuesday.\nTemperatures are also favored to slowly trend upwards after\nearly next week.",
